{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 15,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the executive-employment setting, Lankau’s claimed compensation promises, SOP III’s subsequent approval, and Luxoft’s motion to dismiss."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Understanding sections 4(a) and 4(d) requires ordinary contract interpretation, not technical knowledge of stock-option valuation or securities law."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The disputed language sits in two compensation clauses, with section 14’s merger clause supplying limited context rather than requiring reconstruction across documents."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 68–73 expressly contrast the unambiguous salary discretion with the ambiguous stock-plan approval condition, while making clear that the equity ruling is only at the pleading stage."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Although both disputes concern Board discretion, d01 addresses salary review or adjustment and d02 addresses whether approval attaches to SOP III or Lankau’s individual eligibility."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02: The reviewer must distinguish Board approval of the stock-option plan from approval of Lankau’s individual participation, and recognize that the court accepted both readings as plausible rather than finally establishing an equity entitlement.",
  "reason": "This case is suitable for a generalist reviewer with a short orientation to the employment arrangement and motion-to-dismiss posture. The relevant clauses and lines 68–74 give the competing readings and the court’s treatment directly. The main caution is not to turn the survival of the equity claim into a definitive ruling that Lankau was entitled to stock. The surrounding fraud, securities-law, and termination discussions do not need detailed study to understand the listed defects."
}
